# The question

CI jobs will run on our own EKS nodes either way. The question is who schedules them, where their logs, caches, and secrets live, and what language the pipeline logic is written in. Neither option is a thesis to defend here — each criterion below collects what both sides have to say, and the context section names the conditions that swing the verdict. Node color marks which side an argument serves; the arrows carry the valence.

[Woodpecker + Dagger]: Run Woodpecker CI — a self-hosted server and agents with the Kubernetes backend, one pod per step — as a Flux-managed app, with thin `.woodpecker/*.yaml` files that call Dagger functions written in Go, Python, or TypeScript, executed by an in-cluster Dagger engine. #woodpecker
  -> [GitHub Actions with ARC]

[GitHub Actions with ARC]: Keep GitHub Actions workflows, but execute them on Actions Runner Controller scale sets in our own clusters — GitHub's SaaS control plane queues, routes, logs, and holds secrets; our nodes do the work. #arc
  -> [Woodpecker + Dagger]


# Control-plane dependency and reliability

<ARC moves the compute, not the dependency>: With ARC, job scheduling, log streaming, `actions/cache`, artifacts, and secrets all still run through GitHub's Actions service; when Actions is degraded the scale-set listener receives no jobs and the runners sit idle. Self-hosting the runners does nothing about the outages that started this evaluation. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  - <GitHub is in the path either way>: While repos stay on GitHub, Woodpecker still needs GitHub's webhooks to trigger and its API to post status checks; a GitHub incident that breaks pushes or webhooks stops both systems, so the independence gained is partial. #arc
    - <Partial is the part that hurt>: Actions-specific incidents — queued jobs that never start, runners that never get assigned — are a separate failure domain from git hosting, and that is the domain Woodpecker removes. A delayed webhook can be redelivered or triggered manually; a stalled Actions queue cannot be worked around from our side. #woodpecker

<A self-hosted control plane is ours to break>: Woodpecker's server, its database, and its agents become our availability problem — upgrades, Postgres backups, and a CI outage that we have to diagnose ourselves at 2am. #arc
  +> [GitHub Actions with ARC]
  - <It is one more Flux app on infrastructure we already run>: A stateless Go server backed by a CNPG database is exactly the kind of workload this template already deploys, backs up, and alerts on; it inherits the runbooks rather than requiring new ones. #woodpecker


# Pipeline language and lock-in

<Pipeline logic should be code, not CI YAML>: Dagger functions are typed, testable, and composable in a real language; the Woodpecker YAML shrinks to a trigger and a `dagger call`. Swapping CI engines later means rewriting a few lines rather than every workflow. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  - <Dagger is not tied to Woodpecker>: Nothing stops Actions workflows on ARC from being equally thin `dagger call` wrappers, so the portability belongs to Dagger, not to the runner choice. #arc
    - <Without a forcing function, the YAML grows back>: On Actions the Marketplace is one `uses:` away, and every convenience action pulled into a workflow is logic that lives outside Dagger. A runner with a thin plugin ecosystem keeps the discipline that makes the pipelines portable. #woodpecker

<Workflow syntax is the deepest lock-in>: Expressions, contexts, reusable workflows, and Marketplace actions are GitHub-only; workflow syntax accumulates across every repo and is the asset that never gets migrated. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]

<Existing workflows keep working>: This template already ships `.github/workflows/flux-diff.yaml` with a sticky PR comment posted through `actions/github-script`; on ARC it runs unchanged by pointing `runs-on` at the scale set, while Woodpecker means rewriting it and reimplementing the PR comment against the GitHub API. #arc
  +> [GitHub Actions with ARC]
  - <The rewrite is small and done once>: The workflow's real work is already a shell script (`flux\_diff.sh`) with documented exit codes; only the glue moves, it moves once in this template, and every stamped cluster inherits it. #woodpecker


# Local reproducibility

<The pipeline runs on a laptop unchanged>: `dagger call` runs the same graph locally as in CI, so a failing build is debugged at a terminal rather than by pushing commits and watching logs. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  - <Also available under Actions>: That benefit comes from Dagger, which runs identically inside an ARC runner; the runner is not what makes builds reproducible. #arc
    - <Only for logic that actually lives in Dagger>: Everything still expressed as Actions steps — matrix setup, Marketplace actions, `if:` expressions — reproduces only partially through `act`, if at all. #woodpecker


# Ecosystem and developer experience

<The Marketplace is the largest CI ecosystem>: Thousands of maintained actions, every vendor's docs written Actions-first, and a familiar workflow syntax for any new hire. Woodpecker's plugin set is a fraction of that. #arc
  +> [GitHub Actions with ARC]
  - <Any container is a plugin>: Woodpecker steps are arbitrary images and Dagger modules cover the rest; most Marketplace actions wrap a CLI that can be called directly. #woodpecker
  - <Marketplace actions assume GitHub-hosted images>: Many actions expect the hosted runner's preinstalled toolchain, so on ARC they fail until the runner image is extended — the ecosystem advantage is smaller off GitHub-hosted runners than it looks. #woodpecker

<PR integration is native>: Checks, annotations, job summaries, re-run buttons, and required status checks all live inside the pull request without extra wiring. #arc
  +> [GitHub Actions with ARC]
  - <Status checks cover the gating need>: Woodpecker reports commit statuses that branch protection can require. The UI is plainer, but merges are gated the same way. #woodpecker


# Kubernetes integration

<One pod per step is the native model>: Woodpecker's Kubernetes backend schedules each step as its own pod with its own image and resource requests, which Karpenter can bin-pack. There is no runner image to maintain and no Docker daemon inside a pod. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  - <The Dagger engine is privileged anyway>: Dagger needs a privileged BuildKit-based engine, usually a DaemonSet. The privileged surface moves from a DinD sidecar to the engine pods but does not disappear. #arc
    - <One shared engine beats privilege per job>: A small set of long-lived engine pods on dedicated, tainted nodes is easier to isolate and monitor with Falco than a privileged DinD container in every ephemeral runner. #woodpecker

<Scale sets scale to zero cleanly>: ARC's listener long-polls GitHub for queued jobs and creates runner pods on demand, down to zero, with no metrics plumbing; it is GitHub-maintained and widely deployed. #arc
  +> [GitHub Actions with ARC]
  - <ARC brings its own moving parts>: The controller, a listener per scale set, a runner image, and a choice between DinD (privileged) and Kubernetes mode (container hooks plus a workspace PVC). The ops burden is comparable to running Woodpecker, not lighter. #woodpecker


# Caching and data locality

<Caches and logs stay in the cluster>: Dagger's cache volumes and layer cache live on the engine nodes, and logs live in Woodpecker's database, so build data never leaves the VPC and cache restores happen at local-disk speed. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  - <ARC can keep caches local too>: Running Dagger or BuildKit inside ARC runners gets the same in-cluster layer cache; only `actions/cache` and artifacts cross the internet to GitHub. #arc
    - <Logs and secrets still leave>: Job logs stream to GitHub and secrets are injected from GitHub. Caching can be fixed on ARC; data residency for logs and secrets cannot. #woodpecker


# Supply chain and blast radius

<Third-party actions run inside our VPC>: An action pulled by tag runs with the workflow's token. On ARC it also runs on a node with IRSA or Pod Identity and network reach into the cluster, so a hijacked action tag now lands inside our VPC instead of on GitHub's machine. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  - <Pinning and policy contain it>: SHA-pinned actions (as `flux-diff.yaml` already does), Renovate to bump them, an org allowlist of permitted actions, and NetworkPolicy on runner namespaces cut the risk to the same level as any pinned container image. #arc
    - <Woodpecker gets the same discipline by default>: Steps are images pinned by digest under the same Renovate flow, and there is no second package ecosystem to police. #woodpecker


# Cost and commercial exposure

<The orchestration layer has a price tag now>: GitHub announced a per-minute platform charge for self-hosted runner usage on private repos starting March 2026; reports since then conflict on whether it was postponed or is being billed. In either case, the vendor has shown it will charge for the control plane that ARC depends on. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  - <The fee is small at our scale>: For a team of under ten, the announced rate on a few thousand minutes a month is a rounding error next to the engineering time a migration costs. #arc
    - <The risk is the precedent, not the price>: The cost to worry about is pricing we don't control on a dependency we can't easily leave; a free, self-hosted control plane can't be repriced. #woodpecker


# Forge coupling

<Woodpecker is forge-agnostic>: Woodpecker supports GitHub, GitLab, Gitea, Forgejo, and Bitbucket, so moving repos — to Forgejo, or alongside the Radicle work this template already deploys — doesn't force a CI migration at the same time. #woodpecker
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  - <No forge move is planned>: Optimizing for a hypothetical migration buys flexibility nobody has asked to use, while the cost of switching CI is paid now. #arc


# Cross-cutting context

[GitHub Actions outages are the motivating pain]: If this evaluation exists because queued jobs stall and runs fail on GitHub's side, ARC addresses the wrong layer, and the control-plane criterion decides the comparison. #context
  +> <ARC moves the compute, not the dependency>
  -> <GitHub is in the path either way>

[Repos stay on GitHub indefinitely]: If the forge never moves, Woodpecker's forge independence is worth nothing, and the existing workflows, the Marketplace, and native PR integration all weigh more. #context
  -> <Woodpecker is forge-agnostic>
  +> <Existing workflows keep working>
  +> <PR integration is native>

[The team is small and ops time is the scarcest resource]: With fewer than ten engineers, every self-hosted stateful service competes with product work. That favors whichever option adds less to run, and here the two are close. #context
  +> <A self-hosted control plane is ours to break>
  -> <It is one more Flux app on infrastructure we already run>

[Self-hosted FOSS is a standing preference]: When open-source, self-hosted tooling is a principle rather than just a tie-breaker, a proprietary SaaS control plane counts against ARC regardless of how the other criteria score. #context
  +> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  +> <Workflow syntax is the deepest lock-in>


# A middle path

[Dagger on ARC first]: Keep Actions and ARC as the scheduler for now, but move all pipeline logic into Dagger functions and shrink every workflow to a checkout plus `dagger call`. Swapping in Woodpecker later then means writing new trigger files, not rewriting pipelines. #alternative
  -> [Woodpecker + Dagger]
  -> [GitHub Actions with ARC]
  + <Decouples the two decisions>: The pipeline-language migration and the scheduler migration become separate, reversible steps, and the in-cluster Dagger cache pays off immediately. #alternative
  - <Leaves the actual outage problem in place>: Every Actions control-plane incident still blocks every build during the interim, which may last indefinitely once the urgency fades. #woodpecker
  - <Two systems for a while>: Running a Dagger engine fleet alongside ARC's controller, listeners, and runner images means operating both stacks' moving parts before either is retired. #arc
